## 2. Why the Field Embedding Must Be Handled Carefully

On the special fiber the coefficient field is presented as

```text
F_101[alpha,mu]/(
  alpha^3+12 alpha^2+69 alpha+31,
  mu^2+3
),

gamma = alpha+mu.
```

The residue minimal polynomial of `gamma` is

```text
M(gamma)
= gamma^6+24 gamma^5+89 gamma^4+44 gamma^3
  +33 gamma^2+16 gamma+64.
```

The fixed characteristic-zero lift uses these displayed residues as integer
coefficients of `M`. This integer polynomial is not the exact characteristic-
zero resultant of the `alpha` and `mu` equations. Consequently it would be
incorrect to impose `gamma=alpha+mu` literally modulo `101^2`.

Instead work in

```text
R_2=(Z/101^2 Z)[gamma]/(M).
```

The selected residue roots are

```text
alpha_0 = 23+97 gamma+74 gamma^2+65 gamma^3+33 gamma^4+98 gamma^5,
mu_0    = 78+ 5 gamma+27 gamma^2+36 gamma^3+68 gamma^4+ 3 gamma^5.
```

They satisfy `alpha_0+mu_0=gamma` modulo `101`. Both defining polynomials
have invertible derivatives in the residue field. Ordinary simple-root Hensel
lifting therefore gives unique selected lifts in `R_2`:

```text
alpha = [3356,4137,2296,3095,6598,8683]_gamma,
mu    = [8057,5863, 835,2561,1280,8184]_gamma.
```

Direct multiplication in `R_2` verifies

```text
alpha^3+12 alpha^2+69 alpha+31 = 0,
mu^2+3 = 0.
```

Their sum differs from the chosen integer-lift generator by a multiple of
`101`, exactly as it may:

```text
alpha+mu-gamma
= [1212,9999,3131,5656,7878,6666]_gamma.
```

This selected Hensel embedding, rather than an unjustified equality of
generators, is used throughout the restriction calculation.

